Great catch — that's confusing for everyone at first. Let me break it down.

## The line

```java
this.a = a;
```

You see "a" three times, but they mean **two different things**.

## Let's label them

```java
this . a  =  a ;
//   ↑        ↑
//  #1       #2
```

Wait — that's only 2 different `a`s. The third one you see is the **parameter** `a` in the method line:

```java
EkClass(int a) {
    this.a = a;
    //  ↑   ↑
    // #1  #2
}
```

So there's really only **two `a`s**. Let me explain each.

## The two `a`s

### #1: `this.a` — the **field** (the class variable)
It's the `a` that belongs to the **object**. Declared at the top of the class:

```java
class EkClass {
    int a;              // ← this is "this.a"
    
    EkClass(int a) {    // ← this is the parameter
        this.a = a;     // ← left side is the field, right side is the parameter
    }
}
```

### #2: `a` (right side) — the **parameter**
It's the value **passed in** when you create the object.

```java
new EkClass(5);
//            ↑
//      this "5" goes into the parameter "a"
```

## So the line reads as

```java
this.a = a;
//  ↑     ↑
// field = parameter
// "put the parameter's value INTO the object's field"
```

## Full example with different names to make it obvious

Sometimes it's easier to see when the names are different:

```java
class EkClass {
    int storedValue;                // field (belongs to object)
    
    EkClass(int incomingValue) {    // parameter (given when creating)
        storedValue = incomingValue;
    }
}
```

Same idea. `storedValue = incomingValue;` — put the input into the field.

But when both are named `a`, you need `this` to tell Java which is which.

## Why do we need `this` at all?

Because Java gets confused when two variables have the same name.

```java
EkClass(int a) {
    a = a;      // ❌ Which "a" is which?
                // Java sees this and does nothing!
}
```

`this.a` means **"the field on this object."**
Plain `a` means **"the closest variable named a"** — which is the parameter.

## TL;DR

| Symbol | What it means |
|---|---|
| `this.a` | The **field** — the object's own variable |
| `a` (right side) | The **parameter** — the value passed in |
| `this` | "the object I'm inside right now" |

The line **`this.a = a;`** means:

> **"Take the input `a` and store it in this object's field `a`."**

